{
	// Typecheck for the deletion-gate tests. The build config deliberately
	// excludes `test/` so a test file can never reach the shipped bundle, but
	// that exclusion also meant nothing typechecked the tests, and two costs came
	// due at once:
	//
	//   1. The `@ts-expect-error` assertions guarding the deletion token's
	//      unforgeability were inert — asserting nothing, in the file whose whole
	//      job is proving the token cannot be forged.
	//   2. Adding a required field to `decideMirrorDeletion` left a safety test
	//      passing `undefined` for it, which silently flipped that test from
	//      "refuses an incomplete scan" to "allows" instead of failing to build.
	//
	// Scoped to the gate's own tests rather than all of `test/`: the wider tree
	// has pre-existing errors (untyped `.mjs` imports, un-narrowed unions) whose
	// cleanup is its own change, and a gate that cannot go green gates nothing.
	// Widen this list as those files are cleaned up.
	//
	// The settings row-grammar tests joined for the same reason: their assertions are
	// type-level (an illegal row must fail to compile), and an assertion in an unchecked
	// file asserts nothing — cost 1 above, repeated. The rendering harness and the mock are
	// listed too, which buys their internal consistency and their use of the `src/` types they
	// touch. What it does NOT buy: the mock is a standalone module implementing no declared
	// interface, so `tsc` never compares it against `obsidian.d.ts` — a mock that drifts from
	// the real API still compiles clean. Only production code is checked against the real
	// `.d.ts`, and a drifted mock shows up as tests passing against a fiction.
	"extends": "./tsconfig.json",
	"include": [
		"src/**/*.ts",
		"test/askMirror.test.ts",
		"test/askMirrorGate.adversarial.test.ts",
		"test/catchUp.test.ts",
		"test/settings.test.ts",
		"test/settingsRows.test.ts",
		"test/helpers/settingsTab.ts",
		"test/mocks/obsidian.ts",
		"test/mocks/domAugmentations.ts",
		"plus-pricing.json",
		"mobile-install.json"
	],
	"exclude": ["node_modules"]
}
